# HTML & Component Rules

> **The kit.** `Heading`, `Button`, `Panel`, `Accordion`, `Callout`,
> `ChoiceList`, `RelationIcon`, and the form controls in `@/components/Forms`
> (`Input`, `Textarea`, `Select`, `Checkbox`, `FormError`). All of the sections
> below are binding except **Modal** — there is no `Modal` component yet, so use
> a native `<dialog>` and build one the first time a second place needs it.

## Headings

- **Never use raw `<h1>`, `<h2>`, `<h3>`, `<h4>`, `<h5>` elements** — always use the `<Heading>` component from `@/components/Heading`
- Use `as` for the semantic HTML element and `size` for the visual size — **they are separate decisions**. The document outline is what a screen reader navigates by (one `<h1>`, no skipped levels); the visual scale is a design question and often disagrees
- `Heading` has no `"use client"`, so it renders to HTML with no JavaScript when used from a server component

```tsx
import { Heading } from "@/components/Heading"

// Page title
<Heading as="h1" size="h2">Page Title</Heading>

// Section headings
<Heading as="h2" size="h3">Section Title</Heading>

// Subsection headings
<Heading as="h3" size="h4">Subsection Title</Heading>
```

### Heading Props Reference

| Prop | Type | Default | Description |
|------|------|---------|-------------|
| `as` | `"h1" \| "h2" \| "h3" \| "h4" \| "h5" \| "div"` | `"h2"` | Semantic HTML element |
| `size` | `"hero" \| "h1" \| "h2" \| "h3" \| "h4" \| "h5" \| "base"` | `"h2"` | Visual size |
| `className` | `string` | — | Additional CSS class |
| `uppercase` | `boolean` | `false` | Uppercase text transform |
| `center` | `boolean` | `false` | Center-aligned text |
| `isHidden` | `boolean` | `false` | Visually hidden, still announced |
| `id` | `string` | — | For `aria-labelledby` on the section it titles |

## Buttons

- **Never use raw `<button>` elements** — always use the `<Button>` component from `@/components/Button`
- **The exception is a composite control**: one whose content is a layout rather than a label with an optional icon — a disclosure header (title + count + chevron), a list row that is itself a button (avatar + two lines + dates), a canvas affordance below the UI type scale. `Button` models a labelled action; forcing one of these through it means overriding its size, padding, radius, type *and* colour, which is everything the component contributes. Style those as a raw `<button>` in the owning component's module, and say in a comment why. Current examples: `PersonPanel`'s action row and menu items, `PersonCard`'s reveal bar, `AddSlotCard`
- **`href` decides the element**: with it you get a `next/link` (prefetched, client-side navigation), without it a real `<button>`. Actions get buttons, destinations get links — never the reverse
- **`icon` takes a node, not a name** — `icon={<Plus size={16} />}`. A string resolved through a registry cannot be tree-shaken, so it would pull the whole icon set into the bundle
- **An icon-only button needs `aria-label`** — there is no text to announce
- `Button` has no `"use client"`. Passing `onClick` is what puts it in a client bundle, and only for the component that passes one — a server component cannot pass a function at all, which TypeScript catches at compile time

```tsx
import { Plus, X } from "lucide-react"
import { Button } from "@/components/Button"

// Standard action button
<Button label={t("submit")} variant="primary" onClick={handleSubmit} />

// Button with icon
<Button label={t("addPerson")} variant="secondary" icon={<Plus size={16} />} />

// Link styled as button — renders next/link, no JavaScript from a server component
<Button label={t("settings")} variant="ghost" href="/settings" />

// Icon-only button
<Button icon={<X size={16} />} aria-label={t("close")} variant="ghost" />
```

### Button Props Reference

| Prop | Type | Default | Description |
|------|------|---------|-------------|
| `label` | `string` | — | Button text (omit for icon-only) |
| `variant` | `"primary" \| "secondary" \| "ghost" \| "danger" \| "link"` | `"secondary"` | Visual variant |
| `href` | `string` | — | Renders as `next/link` when provided |
| `isExternal` | `boolean` | `false` | With `href`, renders a plain `<a target="_blank">` |
| `type` | `"button" \| "submit" \| "reset"` | `"button"` | HTML button type |
| `onClick` | `MouseEventHandler` | — | Client components only |
| `icon` | `ReactNode` | — | Icon element, before the label by default |
| `iconAfter` | `ReactNode` | — | Icon element, always after the label |
| `iconPosition` | `"left" \| "right"` | `"left"` | Placement of `icon` |
| `size` | `"sm" \| "md" \| "lg"` | `"md"` | Button size |
| `outlined` | `boolean` | `false` | Drops the fill, promotes it to the border |
| `full` | `boolean` | `false` | Fills the container width |
| `disabled` | `boolean` | `false` | Disabled state |

### Variant roles (this codebase)

| Use | `variant` |
|-----|-----------|
| The main commit on a form or step — save, add, continue | `primary` |
| A secondary action that still needs a visible control | `secondary` |
| Cancel, back, dismiss, low-emphasis chrome | `ghost` |
| Delete and other irreversible actions | `danger` |
| Inline in a sentence, where a button would be too loud | `link` |

`primary` is the app's one inverted surface — ink-on-page in light, page-on-ink
in dark. It follows the theme automatically; do not restyle it per component.

## Empty States

There is no shared `EmptyState` component yet. The pattern to copy is
`components/EmptyTree/EmptyTree.tsx`: a bordered card with an icon badge, a
`<Heading>`, one line of help, and **one** `variant="primary"` action that is
the single path forward — never a bare `<p>` saying "nothing here".

- The action must be the thing that produces the missing data, not a link to
  somewhere else to think about it.
- Every string goes through `useTranslations()`, including the help line.
- Build the shared component the second time this shape is needed.

## Page Structure (`app/` files)

- **Pages should only contain data fetching and wrapper `<div>` elements** — all UI must be delegated to components
- Never put complex HTML structures (grids, lists, cards, forms) directly in page files
- Pages fetch data, transform it, and pass it as props to components
- Wrapper `<div>` elements for layout (e.g., applying a page-level CSS class) are acceptable

```tsx
// Good: page.tsx passes data to components
export default async function BoatPage({ params }) {
  const boat = await fetchBoat(params.id)
  return (
    <div className={styles.page}>
      <BoatHeader name={boat.name} year={boat.year} />
      <BoatSpecs specs={boat.specifications} />
      <BoatAmenities specs={boat.specifications} />
    </div>
  )
}

// Bad: page.tsx contains inline HTML structure
export default async function BoatPage({ params }) {
  const boat = await fetchBoat(params.id)
  return (
    <div>
      <div className={styles.header}>
        <h1>{boat.name}</h1>
        <div className={styles.specGrid}>
          <div className={styles.spec}>
            <span>{boat.specs.length}</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  )
}
```

## Text & Translations

- **Never hardcode user-facing text strings** — always use `useTranslations()` from `next-intl`
- This applies to **all** visible text: labels, headings, placeholders, button text, tooltips, error messages, tab names, menu items, badges, and config arrays with display strings
- When defining config objects/arrays with labels (e.g. tab configs, menu items, filter options), store only keys/IDs and resolve labels via `t()` at render time
- All three locale files (`messages/en.json`, `messages/fr.json`, `messages/es.json`) must be updated when adding new keys

```tsx
import { useTranslations } from "next-intl"

// Good: translated text
const t = useTranslations("Booking")
<Button label={t("submit")} variant="primary" />
<Heading as="h2" size="h3">{t("title")}</Heading>
<p>{t("description")}</p>
<input placeholder={t("searchPlaceholder")} />

// Bad: hardcoded strings
<Button label="Submit" variant="primary" />
<Heading as="h2" size="h3">Booking Details</Heading>
<p>Select your preferred date</p>
```

- For strings with dynamic values, use ICU message format in the JSON files and pass parameters:

```tsx
// messages/en.json: { "Booking": { "pricePerDay": "From {price} per day" } }
t("pricePerDay", { price: boat.price })
```

## Form Inputs

- **Never use raw `<input>` elements** — always use the `<Input>` component from `@/components/Forms`
- **Never use raw `<select>` elements** — always use the `<Select>` component from `@/components/Forms`
- Both components support a `label` prop — use it instead of rendering a separate `<label>` element
- Use the `full` prop to make the field fill its container width
- Use the `id` prop for explicit label association (the component handles `htmlFor` internally)

```tsx
import { Input, Select } from "@/components/Forms"

// Text input with label
<Input type="text" id="name" label={t("nameLabel")} placeholder={t("namePlaceholder")} full />

// Number input with label
<Input type="number" id="price" label={t("priceLabel")} value={price} onChange={handleChange} full />

// Select with label
<Select id="category" label={t("categoryLabel")} value={category} onChange={handleChange} full>
  <option value="a">Option A</option>
  <option value="b">Option B</option>
</Select>

// Bad: raw elements and external labels
<label htmlFor="name">Name</label>
<input type="text" id="name" />

<label htmlFor="category">Category</label>
<select id="category">
  <option value="a">Option A</option>
</select>
```

## Semantic HTML

- Use `<main>` for the primary content area (already in layout.tsx)
- Use `<section>` for distinct content sections, prefer the `<Section>` component from `@/components/Section`
- Use `<nav>` for navigation regions
- Use `<Button>` for actions, `<Link>` for navigation — never the reverse
- **Never wrap a block-level component with an inline element** like `<a>` or `<Link>` — if a card needs to be clickable, pass `link` as a prop instead of wrapping it
- **Always use the `<Modal>` component from `@/components/Modal` for modals, overlays, and confirmation dialogs** — never build dialogs from `<div>` overlays with `onClick` handlers. `<Modal>` wraps the native `<dialog>` element and provides `showModal()`/`close()`, `::backdrop`, Escape key, and outside-click handling out of the box
- All interactive elements must have accessible names (`aria-label`, visible text, or `aria-labelledby`)
